==============================================================================
 hydromate case template - USAGE
==============================================================================
This folder is a ready-to-copy scaffold for one TELEMAC-2D (+ optional GAIA)
simulation case driven by the `hydromate` package. Copy the whole folder to
cases/<your-case>/, drop your data into user-sources/, edit case-config.yml,
then run the three workflow scripts. A worked example is in cases/example-Inn/.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 FOLDER STRUCTURE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
cases/<your-case>/
  case-config.yml      the case configuration (paths, mesh, friction,
                       hydrodynamics, calibration). All paths resolve relative
                       to this file. [edit this]
  preprocessing.py            step 1: build the full TELEMAC case into simulation/
  initial_run.py              step 1b: test-run the built case (confirms it works)
  mesh_convergence_study.py   step 2: grid-independence study + xlsx report
  add3d.py                    optional: write/run the three TELEMAC-3D cases
                              (hotstart3d_hydrostatic.cas, hotstart3d_hydrodyn.cas,
                              unsteady3d.cas) hotstarted from the 2D result
  vertical_convergence_3d.py  optional: 3D grid-independence study over the number
                              of vertical (sigma) layers
  unsteady_run.py             optional: hydrograph (quasi-steady) run hotstarted from
                              the steady result; in-script toggles for 2D/3D + GAIA
                              bedload / suspended load (needs boundaries.inflow series)
  USAGE.info                  this file

  user-sources/        YOUR input data (gitignored; not shipped with the template)
    geodata/           DEM(s), ROI polygon, liquid boundaries, mesh zones,
                       channel centerline, roughness zones (+ table), rating curve
    ground-truth/      field measurements (e.g. FlowTracker velocities, GSDs)
    hydraulics/        inflow discharge series / value
    (see user-sources/README.md for the expected files)

  tm-simulation/       ALL produced artifacts (created on first run; gitignored),
                       split by workflow phase - each phase also writes its own
                       hydromate.log here:
    preprocessing/         DEM clips, mesh-raw.slf, mesh-elevations.slf,
                           ground-truth.xlsx
    simulation/            the TELEMAC case: geometry.slf, boundaries.cli,
                           steady2d.cas, friction.tbl, results
    postprocessing/
      mesh-convergence/    convergence study: mesh-convergence.xlsx/.txt, per-mesh
                           runs, log (this subfolder is created by preprocessing.py)
    calibration-validation/  HydroBayesCal artifacts: measurements-calibration.csv,
                           config_Telemac.py

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PRINCIPAL WORK STEPS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. Install the env and activate it (see docs/installation.rst):
     mamba env create -f environment.yml && mamba activate hydromate-env
     pip install -e .

1. Put your data in user-sources/ (EPSG:25832 / a metric CRS; see
   user-sources/README.md and the "Coordinate system" note in CLAUDE.md).

2. Edit case-config.yml:
     - telemac.pysource  -> your TELEMAC pysource.*.sh
     - inputs.*          -> your files under user-sources/
     - mesh / friction / hydrodynamics / calibration -> your values
   No measured outlet rating curve? Synthesise one from normal-flow hydraulics:
     hydromate rating -o user-sources/geodata/rating-curve.csv \
       --manning 0.035 --slope <S0> --width <b> --bed-elevation <z> --q <Q>

3. Validate the configuration:
     hydromate cases/<your-case>/case-config.yml --check

4. Step 1 - build the complete TELEMAC case into tm-simulation/simulation/
   (final mesh, .cli, .tbl, .cas; the inflow + outflow rating are synthesised
   from the geodata if missing; HBC artifacts -> calibration-validation/):
     python cases/<your-case>/preprocessing.py

5. Step 1b - test-run the built case once to confirm it does not crash, check
   the boundary-flux convergence (extracted-fluxes.csv + plots),
   and - once the fluxes stay balanced (<1e-3 m3/s over 10 printouts, or in the
   10-printout mean) - generate hotstart2d.cas (a continuation of r2d.slf capped
   at that steady time, Q/H prescriptions kept). This ends the preprocessing
   step; needs telemac.pysource set:
     python cases/<your-case>/initial_run.py

6. Step 2 - mesh-convergence (grid-independence) study: runs five meshes
   (+40%/+20% coarser, baseline, -20%/-40% finer) and writes a styled
   mesh-convergence.xlsx with a recommended cell size (convergence vs. compute):
     python cases/<your-case>/mesh_convergence_study.py

7. Step 3 - calibrate / validate with HydroBayesCal using the emitted config:
     cd cases/<your-case>/tm-simulation/calibration-validation
     python /path/to/hydrobayescal/bal_telemac.py --config config_Telemac.py

The 2D path above is the foundation. A 3D simulation is built ONLY after it - it
needs the 2D hotstart (step 1b -> r2d.slf) and reuses the horizontal mesh whose
resolution the 2D mesh-convergence study has already validated (step 2). So run the
two optional 3D steps only after steps 1b and 2:

Optional 3D-a - 3D extension: write exactly three TELEMAC-3D cases hotstarted
from the 2D result, with the number of sigma layers and the turbulence model
inferred from r2d.slf and a fixed time step sized for Courant 0.6:
  hotstart3d_hydrostatic.cas  NON-HYDROSTATIC VERSION : NO; constant Q/H, ~30k
                              fixed steps, short listing period -> the steady
                              boundary-flux convergence check in 3D
  hotstart3d_hydrodyn.cas     NON-HYDROSTATIC VERSION : YES; steady run with the
                              in-file prescribed Q (in) and H (out)
  unsteady3d.cas              NON-HYDROSTATIC VERSION : YES; hydrograph Q(t) +
                              outflow SL(t) via the same liquid-boundaries file
                              as unsteady2d.cas (needs a varying
                              boundaries.inflow series; skipped otherwise)
(--run [hydrostatic|hydrodyn|unsteady] also launches telemac3d.py; default is
the hydrostatic flux-convergence check):
     python cases/<your-case>/add3d.py [--run [which]]

Optional 3D-b - vertical-layer convergence: the vertical discretization dz (number
of sigma layers) is a NEW discretization choice the 2D study never covered, so it
needs its own grid-independence study (the 3D analogue of step 2). This re-runs the
hotstarted 3D case for a ladder of vertical-layer counts on the same horizontal mesh
and reports grid independence vs. the number of layers (styled .xlsx in
tm-simulation/postprocessing/vertical-convergence/). Runs telemac3d once per count
and is slow:
     python cases/<your-case>/vertical_convergence_3d.py

   (A one-shot build without the scripts: hydromate cases/<your-case>/case-config.yml)

Logging: every script writes a compound, timestamped hydromate.log into the
folder it outputs to, with the elapsed time of each calculation step plus all
warnings and errors.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 WHERE TO READ MORE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  README.md                 overview, pipeline stages, case layout, CLI
  docs/installation.rst     environment + TELEMAC setup
  OPTIONAL OpenFOAM free-surface (VOF) extension - after step 1b, and
  independent of the TELEMAC-3D one. For when the vertical structure matters
  AND the water surface has a gradient (which rules out simpleFoam):

    hydromate openfoam case-config.yml --check   # cell count, no build
    python openfoam_preprocessing.py             # build the interFoam case
    python openfoam_run.py                       # spin-up, run, discharge report

  Needs an `openfoam:` block in case-config.yml (commented out in the template)
  with `bashrc` pointing at your OpenFOAM etc/bashrc. The mesh is a structured,
  terrain-following, all-hexahedral grid written directly to constant/polyMesh -
  no snappyHexMesh - and the fields are seeded from the converged r2d.slf.

  STRUCTURES (dams, weirs, walls, buildings) - optional, shared by both solvers

    Draw them in QGIS as an ordinary vector layer. NO STL, no CAD: a polygon is
    the footprint, or a line + a Width (m) is buffered into one. Give each a
    Crest (m) for a level crest (dam, weir, floodwall) or a Height (m) to follow
    the terrain (embankment, levee).

      overflow  dam/weir/levee    -> the BED IS RAISED to the crest (both solvers)
      solid     wall/building     -> OpenFOAM REMOVES the footprint (real vertical
                                     walls); TELEMAC raises the bed to crest +
                                     solid_freeboard_2d instead

    Point geodata.structures at the layer and uncomment the structures: block in
    case-config.yml.

  WHAT CAN THIS CASE DO?

    hydromate status case-config.yml          # summary, refreshes the markers
    hydromate status case-config.yml --full   # the full capability table

  Each solver gets a MODEL=<SOLVER>_<ENABLED|DISABLED> file here. The NAME says
  whether this case declares that solver; the BODY reports every capability on
  three axes - implemented (does hydromate support it for that solver at all),
  configured (does this case ask for it), and built/run (do the artifacts exist).
  The files are generated on every build, so do not edit them.

  docs/usage.rst            CLI, GUI editor, example scripts, inputs
  CLAUDE.md                 in-depth design notes (meshing, boundaries,
                            friction, ground truth, conventions)
  cases/example-Inn/        a complete, filled-in worked example
  hydromate <config> -h     command-line help (also: hydromate rating -h)
